--- !u!114 &1509291756
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1509291755}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2330b6f59de4c2349ad2089aeb64866f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::UIPromptController
  pages:
  - pageName: Page 1
    pageText: In this experiment, we shall study the effect of variation in the concentration
      of iodide ions on the rate of reaction of iodide ions with hydrogen peroxide
      at room temperature.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 2
    pageText: Tap on each of the labels to understand its role in the experiment.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 3
    pageText: To begin with, we shall add 5 mL, 10 mL, 15 mL and 20 mL of 0.1 M KI
      to each labelled conical flask.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 4
    pageText: 
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 1674447114}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 5
    pageText: Now, we will add 10 mL of 2.5 M H2SO4 to each flask.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 6
    pageText: 
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 1099346183}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 7
    pageText: Let us now bring the solution up to 100 mL by adding 85 mL, 80 mL,
      75 mL and 70 mL distilled water to flasks 'A', 'B', 'C'and 'D' respectively.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 8
    pageText: 
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 311906199}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 9
    pageText: Now, we will add 10 mL Na2S2O3 solution to each of the four conical
      flasks.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 10
    pageText: 
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 1537638142}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 11
    pageText: Now, we must add 5 mL of starch solution that will act as an indicator
      to each flask with a measuring cylinder.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 12
    pageText: Drag the beaker containing the freshly prepared starch solution near
      the 50 mL measuring cylinder.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 13
    pageText: To maintain the solutions at room temperature, the conical flasks must
      be placed in a water bath.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 14
    pageText: We will add 3% H2O2 solution to each of the conical flasks to carry
      out the reaction. But, for now, let us observe what will happen inside the
      flask if 3% H2O2 solution is added to it.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 15
    pageText: The reaction will commence only after the addition of 3% hydrogen peroxide
      solution into the reaction mixture.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 16
    pageText: First, the iodide ions are oxidised to iodine molecules by hydrogen
      peroxide in an acidic medium.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 17
    pageText: Drag the following species to the blanks to form a balanced chemical
      equation for the oxidation of iodide ions.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 18
    pageText: The solution contains thiosulphate ions that act as a reducing agent.
      Drag the iodine molecule towards any of the thiosulphate ions.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 19
    pageText: Drag the following species to the blanks to form a balanced chemical
      equation.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 20
    pageText: 'MCQ

'
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 21
    pageText: Once all the thiosulphate ions react with the iodine (l2) molecules,
      the concentration of iodine liberated in the reaction of hydrogen peroxide
      with iodide ions increases rapidly.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 22
    pageText: As iodine is less soluble in water, it reacts with I- ions present
      in the solution, to form triiodide ions. This makes a linear triiodide ion
      complex.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 23
    pageText: Now, this triiodide ions reacts with the starch (amylose) to form a
      blue-black coloured complex. This is due to the triiodide ion slipping inside
      the amylose coil/helical structure.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 24
    pageText: MCQ
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 0}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 25
    pageText: To commence the reaction in the flasks, add 5 mL of 3% H2O2 and record
      the time taken for the blue colouration in the solution.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 26
    pageText: Drag the reagent bottle containing 3% H2O2 near the 50 mL measuring
      cylinder.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 27
    pageText: H2O2, a highly reactive chemical, should always be kept in a closed
      brown bottle to avoid its contact with air or moisture.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 28
    pageText: Click on the measuring cylinder to transfer 5 mL of 3% H2O2 solution
      into each conical flask.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 29
    pageText: We can observe that different solutions take different amounts of time
      for the completion of the reaction, which is seen by the appearance of blue
      colour at different times.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 30
    pageText: Drag the time from the respective stopwatch to the observation table.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 31
    pageText: A The time taken by the solution to consume all the thiosulphate ions
      by iodine is reproducible, and the time for the appearance of blue colour is
      noted. Hence, this 30mL -125 100 The more the concentration of the iodide ions,
      the higher is the rate of the reaction.  B  reaction is also known as clock
      reaction.  265  L75  1
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 32
    pageText: 'CONCLUSION

'
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels: []
